ibility  to address this in the recovery well  let's talk to professor sugar about  schools  shall we because different nations are  doing slightly different things here uh  aren't they so  in wells and scotland younger pupils  returning from the 22nd of february  in england and northern ireland schools  closed until at least the 8th of march  when should school start reacting  so i think what's absolutely clear is  that children have borne the brunt of  this pandemic and there are massive  harms to children being out of school  and schools should be  the first things to open and the last  things to close  even if we have to keep in place  restrictions in other places  and there is also an age gradient we've  learned about which is we've seen  much fewer outbreaks in nurseries and  we've seen it go up so the  older the age cohort the more outbreaks  you seem to see  so we're trying to do in scotland is  22nd of february get the youngest kids  back  early years p1 to p3 and then  you get them back to some normality  assess that and then cautiously start  expanding out to get kids back  and one of the great things that we saw  last summer was because we managed to  squash the numbers really to elimination  levels  you know we had few cases when we opened  schools 76 percent of schools  you know had no cases until you know  mid-october november  and and of the schools that had cases it  was just one  case and then test and trace came in and  was able to isolate that child without  it spreading  so there is a way to run schools  effectively but it does require  suppression so you don't keep having  cases in school  right now the restrictions are in place  the numbers are coming down let's hope  we can get schools open  okay have you been on a bit of a journey  yourself i just wonder because you know  you talked a lot at the beginning of  last year about  looking down about zero covered about  suppressing the virus and it feels more  recently  you've been talking a bit m